[Event "Knight School"]
[Opening "Danish Gambit"]
[Variation "Danish Gambit Accepted: Schlechter Defense"]
[ECO "C21"]
[Character "gambit,tactical"]
[Risk "medium"]
[EarlyMate "none"]
[Tricks "trap"]
[Themes "queen-trade,piece-activity"]
[Kind "sideline"]

1. e4 e5 2. d4 exd4 3. c3 dxc3 4. Bc4 cxb2 5. Bxb2 d5 (5... Nc6 6. Nf3 { Develops and defends e4 while the bishops keep pointing at the kingside. } 6... Bb4+ 7. Nc3 Nf6 8. O-O Bxc3 9. Bxc3)
6. Bxd5 { Takes the pawn that attacked the bishop and keeps the bishop aimed at f7. Black cannot take back with the queen, because Qxd5 then wins it. }
6... Nf6 7. Bxf7+ { Takes a second pawn with check, so the pawns are level again. The king has to recapture, and the queen trade that follows is good for White. }
7... Kxf7 8. Qxd8 { Takes the black queen. Black cannot recapture at once, because the black king is not protected and Black first needs to give check. }
8... Bb4+ 9. Qd2 { The only move: it blocks the check and offers a queen trade, so White keeps the material it won. }
9... Bxd2+ 10. Nxd2 { Recaptures with the knight and keeps the king at home. The pawns are level, the black king is stuck on f7 and White's bishop on b2 is strong. }
10... Rd8 11. Ngf3 { Develops the last knight toward e5, where it hits f7 and d7. Plan: castle long, put a rook on the d-file and use the bishop on b2 along the long diagonal. } 11... Kg8 12. Ne5 { Puts the knight on a strong central square where it cannot be kicked by a pawn. } *
